From: 
	MJ Watson <mjwatson@snafu.livenet.net>
We had to do this last month, as it happens.

It looks like you might be running too many files for the BMT to
be able to easily handle.  The solution involves deleting the
existing domain and recreating it with "mkfdmn -x" or "-p".

bf probably stands for bitmap file (which is what the BMT is, I 
guess).
----------------------------------------------------------------
From:
	"Dr. Tom Blinn, 603-884-0646" <tpb@doctor.zk3.dec.com>
	UNIX Software Group + Compaq Computer Corporation
	
Welcome to the world of AdvFS root file systems and AdvFS's quota
handling.
Even though you don't have quotas enabled on your root file system, AdvFS
insists on checking them for you, and when it finds inconsistencies (as it
almost always does eventually), it insists on reporting them.  "bf" is the
AdvFS bitmap file, I believe, but it's not particularly important, what
you
see is a side effect of using AdvFS for the root file system.

I personally NEVER encourage anyone to use AdvFS for the root file system,
and it's arguable whether it has any real advantages for the /usr file
system
either.

Tom
